hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in his analysis  the challenge of cultural relativism  James Rachel's is going to first set out  what he takes to be a central type of  argument made by those who espouse and  advocate for cultural relativism in  whatever form we happen to find it and  he talks first about a sort of very  specific argument that gets made and  I'll just run you through the two  examples that he has here's one the  premise is the Greeks believed it was  wrong to eat the dead whereas the  collations believed it was right to eat  the dead and the conclusion of that is  therefore eating the dead is neither  objectively right nor objectively wrong  is merely a matter of opinion which  varies from culture to culture and he  could have framed that as it's merely a  matter of cultural norms which vary from  culture to culture as well the next  example that he gives in this piece has  to do with the Eskimo or Inuit I says  that they saw nothing wrong with  infanticide whereas Americans believed  infanticide is immoral and the  conclusion to that would be infanticide  is neither objectively right nor  objectively wrong it's merely a matter  of a  which varies from culture to culture you  notice what the key connecting point is  there right acts whatever cultural  practice that happens to be is neither  objectively right nor objectively wrong  it is merely a matter of opinion or we  could say feeling or habit whatever we  want to call it we could invoke William  Graham summer and the folkways or  anything like that  Ruth Benedict any of the anthropologists  we'd like and we'd have the same  conclusion coming about we can't say any  of this is objectively right or wrong  it's merely something that varies from  culture to culture and for that reason  whatever is going to be right is right  only within a particular culture now  Rachel's generalizes this as indeed do  many cultural relativists those that he  has in his crosshairs in this this essay  into a more wide argument in terms of  its scope where we can say we have the  single premise and then we have a  conclusion the premise different  cultures have different moral codes and  then the conclusion which I've only  partially written up here therefore  there is no objective truth and morality  right and wrong are only matters of  opinion and opinions vary from culture  to culture so since they vary from  culture to culture whatever is right  varies from culture to culture as well  whatever is wrong varies from culture to  culture as well now we should be a  little bit leery of this to begin with  when we see one premise and one  conclusion if we know anything about  argumentation theory because you really  almost never as far as I could think  have a good conclusion coming a  supported conclusion coming from a  single premise so with that shows us  although Rachel's doesn't really talk  about this here is there have to be some  others supporting implicit premises that  are being assumed either on the part of  the person making the argument or the  person  hearing the argument in order for this  to be viewed as a good argument and  Rachel's points out that on its face  this is just a bad argument now he uses  the word unsound which is technically  not the right term to use in this a  unsound argument would be one whose  structure was in fact valid but the  premises are false in this case the  premise is true but we're not sure if  the conclusion actually is true and it  certainly doesn't seem to follow from  the premises we call this kind of  argument invalid why do we call it  invalid it's supposed to be a deductive  argument and just on the basis of its  structure the way inference is coming  about the the premise doesn't  necessarily lead to the conclusion  notice Rachel's says in this that the  conclusion could be true but the way  this is set up this doesn't this does  not provide us good reasons to think  that the conclusion would be true  because of the argument structure and  evidence being provided and he calls us  the cultural differences argument he  says to many people it's persuasive but  it's it's not a good argument so he  gives us a counter example he says  consider the example of the Greeks and  the Galatians the Greeks believed it was  wrong to eat the dead the collations  believed it was right does it follow  from the mere fact that they disagreed  that there is no objective truth in the  matter it doesn't follow he says it  could be that one practice in fact was  objectively right or wrong and one was  simply mistaken now we would need some  sort of frame of reference in order to  say which one was objectively right or  wrong but here the the relativist is  going further and saying we can't  possibly determine whether one of them  is right or wrong except within its own  field Rachel's then invites us to think  about a similar manner he's drawing an  analogy here he says in some societies  Polly people believe the earth is flat  as a matter of fact even within our  Western highly technological society  there is a Flat Earth Society and there  are apparently more and more people  belonging to this year after year so  even in our own complex Western late  modern society there's some people who  believe that the world is flat says in  other societies such as our own people  believe that the earth is a sphere by  the way just as a side note the  medievals did not think that the world  was flat you can see this by actually  reading medieval thinkers like Ansem or  Thomas Aquinas who casually mentioned  the globe of the earth right so now how  do you tell who's right or who's wrong  about this but we don't don't worry  about so much the decision procedure but  would you say that it's just a matter of  opinion the over here they think the  world is flat and because they think  it's flat it is flat over here they  think the world is round and because  they think it's rounded is round that  doesn't work and so he says we wouldn't  buy that we would never draw such a  conclusion because we realized the  members of some societies might simply  be wrong  when it comes to geography and he  invites us to draw a similar conclusion  when it comes to ethics or moral codes  or practices that that people buy into  this is not the strongest argument on  its face that Rachel's is making against  cultural relativism here but he is  pointing something out that if this is  going to be a good argument something  more needs to be added to it and it's  not generally being added to it then he  makes another very interesting  counter-argument himself in the next  section what follows from cultural  relativism he says well let's pretend  that cultural relativism is true what  would follow if it really was true he  says here's the three consequences that  would follow from this one would be that  we cannot say that the customs of other  societies are in any way inferior to our  own or rather we're free to do so but  we're basically just asserting ourselves  and there is no validity to what we're  actually saying and there's no reason  why they should listen to us or anybody  else should listen to us  we're just babbling essentially so long  as we're outside of our little sphere in  what we're judging now that could be  problematic he says that this is indeed  one of the main points stressed by  cultural relativism so the cultural  relativism yeah exactly  so shut up about you know American or  European or Western society or pick  whatever you have being better than  anyone else if you're Chinese quit  saying Chinese society is better than  anyone else now he points out if that's  the case we would also be barred from  criticizing less benign practices so he  brings up tienamin square we want to say  that it's not okay to crush dissidents  and send them into hiding for decades on  end or if we could think about other  things going on more recently that we  might condemn this would mean that we  couldn't condemn them we could condemn  things within our own society but we  couldn't condemn anything outside of our  society we're gonna say well that's  that's just their norms what else we  also can't criticize our own moral code  if that's the case so maybe we can't  actually criticize anything within our  society he says relativism suggests a  simple test for determining what is  right and what's wrong  all we need to do is ask whether the  action is in line or the code of the  society in question so you know although  the caste system has been you know long  sense legally banished  in India it still exists it's actually  such a problem that Britain recently had  to enact laws for workplaces saying that  you can't that that you cannot  discriminate in British companies and on  British soil you cannot discriminate on  the basis of caste because it is such a  pervasive part of culture so he says  suppose a resident of India wonders  whether the country's caste system is  morally correct all they have to do is  ask whether this conforms to the  society's moral code and if that was a  case there never would have been any  sort of getting rid of the caste system  because it was part of the country's  moral code goes all the way back to you  know ancient religious texts and customs  and all sorts of other developments and  we could think of many other things like  that in our own Western society where  nothing would ever have have been  changed we would simply consult you know  what are the norms and then we'd have to  follow them the mere fact that we can  actually call the norms into question  seems to be a problem for the cultural  relativist position the third issue  which is very interesting is that if we  accept cultural relativism we can no  longer talk in terms of progress or  declined we can talk about changes but  we can't say that anything actually  constitutes getting better as opposed to  getting worse in terms of moral norms so  what would that preclude that would mean  for example that we would not be able to  say that the civil rights struggle on  the whole was a sign of moral progress  or certainly the civil rights  legislation as well we would not be able  to say things like we ought to reform  our prisons so that they're more humane  because you know it's just our norms to  treat prisoners badly for the most part  here in America by comparison to say  European countries or we could talk  about drug legalization perhaps it's  progress to legalize certain drugs to  decriminalize them but we wouldn't be  able to say that from the perspective  that is afforded to us of cultural  relativism so here's the upshot of this  [Music]  he says that these three consequences  have led many people to reject it  because cultural relativism implies that  the judgments that we make would make no  sense cultural relativism itself must be  wrong there has to be something wrong  with it so there's an argument here for  cultural relativism and then there's  Rachel's to attempt to rebut that  argument by providing counter arguments  you
 

